    ## Routes  The API uses the path `/api` for all routes. root path can be different on depending on the deployments reverse proxy configuration, consult the deployment specific documentation if needed.  - `api/bts` - endpoints for managing the basestations - `api/trace` - endpoints for managing the traces - `api/tracers` - endpoint for querying the availability of the tracers  ### BTS  Basestations can be registered to the system and some basic info is returned for them via the api, this info can used to check tracer BTS compatibility.  Adding basestations on the system is optional. Trace can be started without registering basestations first. Recommendation is still to register basestations first to sanity check that they are available and which traces can be started on them.  ### Trace  UTF Lab supports multiple simultaneous traces to multiple basestations, but only one trace can target a specific basestation at a time. You may start a trace with multiple target basestations or start multiple traces with multiple targets.  #### Trace profiles  Trace profiles define tracers that should be included to the trace. Predefined trace profiles can be obtained from the api or trace profile can be formed by client. UTF Lab will assign compatible tracers to basestations based on the basestation type. Client cannot override basestation type as it's verified from the basestation on startup.  Predefined trace profiles can be obtained via route `GET /api/trace/v2/profiles/<gnb|enb|bts|vdu|vcu>`  #### Trace status  Single trace status can be obtained via `GET api/trace/v2/<traceID>`  Trace status is represented by finite number of states, visible on the `status` field. Statuses can be  to four general stages for the trace lifecycle. Statuses and stages could be skipped due trace errors.   - Startup `['initializing', 'starting']`     - Basic information is collected from the basestations for the trace     - Users trace request is validated with the info from the basestations     - Start is performed to each tracer base station combo; `traceTarget`   - Running state `['running']`     - Monitoring running trace, retries issued when possible for failed `traceTargets`     - React to user request for trace state change like stop or abort   - Teardown `['stopping', 'packaging']`     - Each traceTarget is stopped     - Log files are collected from each tracer for the particular trace     - Log files are archived to a single zip file   - Done `['failed', 'failedToStop', 'ready', 'aborted']`     - Trace is finished, zip archive is ready for download, all basestation connections are closed.  #### TraceTarget  When trace is started profiles and basestations are formatted to a list of trace targets by basestation type and supported tracer types present on the profile. TraceTarget count and combinations are determined at trace startup after connecting to BTS.  TraceTarget has it's own finite set of states. Status is updated during the trace.  TraceTarget status and trace status have no strict relation. TraceTarget status should not be used for determining trace success or failure.  Up-to-date tracetarget are part of the trace object.  #### Trace dry-run  Trace start with profiles and basestations can be tested on endpoint `POST ​/api​/trace​/v2​/check-preconditions`.  This will run all precondition checks before without starting tracers. If trace cannot be started with given profile(s) and basestations error and correction suggestions are returned. For example error will be returned if tracer requires active cells but basestations does not have any, or if the basestation software version requirement is not met for the tracer.  #### Results  When trace is completed trace results zip archive can be obtained via `GET /api/trace/v2/<traceId>/results`  ### Tracer  `Tracer` is either internal of external process that collects files from the basestation. Tracer availability can be different between deployments as tracers can be adjusted in the UTF Lab server configuration. It's recommended to check available tracers and their requirements before starting a new trace.  ## Example usage  ### Running a trace  #### Register BTS to system (optional)  Register new BTS [register BTS](#operation/CreateBtsV2)  Response contains details of the basestation, verify that the BTS is correct and BTS supports the profile. All basestations support the BTS typed tracers.  #### Select profile  Fetch predefined profiles based on supported tracers for the BTS [all profiles for BTS type](#operation/GetProfilesBtsV2), or format custom profile with information from the [all enabled tracers](#operation/GetTracersV2)  #### Dry run trace (optional)  Test BTS and profile selection with [precondition check](#operation/CheckTracePreconditionsV2), correct any issues based on the result.  #### Start trace  Start trace with [new trace](#operation/NewTraceV2)  #### Poll trace status  Get trace status with trace id [trace by id](#operation/GetTraceInfoByIdV2) until results are available.  #### Download results  Download results with trace id [result by trace id](/#operation/GetResults)  ## Versioning  All routes/endpoints are versioned (e.g. `/api/trace/v1|v2|v3/...`) so if there are backwards incompatible changes to api new route or endpoint is created with new version.  When there is multiple versions available for same route, old ones are marked as deprecated per openapi specification. Old versions are completely removed when their support ends. Consult release notes before using new version of the UTF Lab api.  ### Non-breaking api changes - New keys to existing json objects on mutating requests, like post/put/delete, are not considered breaking when they do not change existing behavior of the api. - New keys on the get request json objects are not considered breaking changes if old properties are not changed. - get response content is not considered as a breaking change if openapi definition does not change.  ## Authentication  UTF Lab does not handle authentication nor authorization, login requirement can be enforced by deployment, usually by combination of Keycloak and Kong. Consult the deployment specific documentation.   # noqa: E501

